D = "ivx_architecture"
ITEMS = [
dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan four stages", m="ask", l=2,
q="Sullivan divided the psychiatric interview into four stages. A trainee spends 40 minutes on a detailed symptom checklist with a new patient and only then asks who the patient is and what brought him. In Sullivan’s terms, which stage did the trainee skip or invert?",
a="Reconnaissance, the rough sketch of who the person is and their life course, which should precede the detailed inquiry",
ae="Sullivan’s sequence is formal inception, reconnaissance, detailed inquiry and termination. Reconnaissance gives the interviewer a rough social and developmental outline of the person, so the detailed inquiry can be aimed at what matters in this life rather than run as a generic checklist. Running the detailed inquiry first means you ask hundreds of questions without knowing which ones carry weight.",
w=[("Formal inception, the explicit statement of why the interviewer is there and what is expected",
"Formal inception is the opening: the greeting, the reason for the meeting, and how the interviewer will proceed. The trainee may have done this perfectly well. The problem lies in what came next: detail before outline."),
("Termination, the summary statement, prescription and formal leave-taking at the end",
"Termination is real and often neglected, but the vignette describes an error at the start of the interview, not the end."),
("Detailed inquiry, the stage in which the interviewer tests hypotheses about the person’s difficulties",
"The detailed inquiry is exactly what the trainee did. The error is that it came first, with no reconnaissance to steer it, so it was not hypothesis testing at all, only data collection.")],
x="Sullivan’s whole method treats the interview as a progressive narrowing of hypotheses. Reconnaissance gives a provisional map; the detailed inquiry checks it; termination hands the patient something useful. Reversing the order turns the interviewer into a questionnaire. Modern descendants of this logic include Shea’s scouting period and Morrison’s free-speech period at the start of the first interview.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview (1954)",
h="Sullivan’s four: “In, Recon, Detail, End” — Inception, Reconnaissance, Detailed inquiry, termination. You scout the land before you dig the well."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan termination components", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="Sullivan described specific elements of a proper interview termination. Which set best matches his account?",
a="A summary statement of what was learned, a prescription for action, an assessment of likely consequences, and formal leave-taking",
ae="Sullivan insisted the patient should leave with something: a clear summary of what the interviewer has understood, a recommendation of what to do, an honest sense of what that may lead to, and a formal, respectful ending. He saw the end of an interview as a moment of great influence, when a patient is most receptive to a durable message.",
w=[("A review of symptoms, a mental status recap, a risk statement, and a scheduled follow-up appointment",
"These are reasonable modern closing tasks, but they are a documentation checklist, not Sullivan’s interpersonal account of what the patient should take away."),
("An interpretation of transference, a test of the patient’s response, a revision, and an open invitation to return",
"Interpreting transference in a first interview was not part of Sullivan’s termination. He warned against leaving the patient more anxious and confused than on arrival."),
("Reassurance, a sincere compliment, a handshake, and a reminder that the patient can call at any time",
"Generic reassurance is what Sullivan wanted to avoid. He wanted the patient to leave with a usable understanding, not just comfort.")],
x="A useful modern translation: (1) “Here is what I understand so far”; (2) “Here is what I suggest”; (3) “Here is what you may notice if you do it, and what it may cost”; (4) a deliberate goodbye. Patients who receive this structure recall the plan and attend follow-up more reliably than patients who are simply thanked and dismissed.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview (1954), chapter on termination",
h="Sullivan’s exit: S-P-A-L = Summary, Prescription, Assessment of consequences, Leave-taking. “Send People Away Learning.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan transitions", m="say", l=2,
q="You are moving from a patient’s work history to the topic of sexual functioning. Sullivan described three kinds of transitions. Which is the accented transition?",
a="“I’d like to turn now to a different area that I ask everyone about, which is sex. How has that side of life been?”",
ae="An accented transition marks the shift explicitly, so the patient is not startled and the interviewer signals that the new topic is routine. Sullivan used accented transitions for topics where an unmarked jump would provoke anxiety or a sense of being trapped.",
w=[("“You mentioned your marriage feels distant at work too. How has intimacy been between you?”",
"This is a smooth transition: it builds a bridge from something the patient said. Smooth transitions are elegant, but they can feel like a trap on sensitive topics, as if the interviewer engineered the link."),
("“How is your sex life?”, asked straight after a question about salary",
"This is an abrupt transition. Sullivan used abrupt transitions deliberately at times, for example to cut through a security operation or to observe the patient’s response to surprise, but not as the default way into a shame-laden topic."),
("“Let’s pause here. Is there anything about your work you’d like to add before we stop?”",
"This is a catch-all closure of the work topic, not a transition into sex.")],
x="Sullivan’s three transitions are smooth, accented and abrupt. Each is a tool. Smooth transitions keep the flow; accented ones protect against anxiety while announcing routine; abrupt ones can disarm defensive maneuvering, but they raise anxiety and should be chosen consciously. Carlat and Shea later described closely related transition types for the modern interview.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview (1954); Carlat, The Psychiatric Interview",
h="Smooth = bridge. Accented = signpost (“I’m turning now”). Abrupt = cliff jump. Use the signpost for shame."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx shea scouting period", m="ask", l=2,
q="Shea describes the first several minutes of the interview as a “scouting” period. What is the interviewer primarily doing there?",
a="Letting the patient talk relatively freely while engaging them and observing what they emphasize, how they speak, and where the interview must go",
ae="In the scouting period the interviewer uses open-ended questions and facilitators, gathers a sense of the patient’s concerns and style, and builds engagement. It is diagnostic reconnaissance and alliance building in one. Closing down to symptom checklists too early costs both information and rapport.",
w=[("Completing the mental status examination before any history is taken",
"The mental status is observed from the first moment, but the scouting period is not a formal MSE. Formal cognitive testing early on can feel intrusive and damage engagement."),
("Establishing the diagnosis with closed questions so the rest can be spent on treatment",
"This is the classic error of premature closure. Shea’s point is the opposite: delay closed questioning until you know where the important regions are."),
("Taking the full past psychiatric and family history in chronological order",
"Chronological history is part of the body of the interview, not the scouting period.")],
x="Shea’s facilic approach divides the interview into introduction, opening (scouting), body, closing and termination. The scouting period lets the interviewer judge how structured to be: a talkative, tangential patient needs early gentle structure; a guarded patient needs more open space and less pressure. The scouting phase is where that decision is made.",
s="Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.)",
h="Scout before you march: listen to the terrain for a few minutes, then plan the route."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx shea facilics referred gate", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="Earlier, a patient said, almost in passing, that her father “drank too much.” Twenty minutes later you want to enter the region of her own alcohol use. In Shea’s facilics, which transition is a referred gate?",
a="“A while ago you mentioned your father drank too much. I’m wondering how alcohol has played a role in your own life.”",
ae="A referred gate uses something the patient said earlier in the interview as the doorway into a new region. It feels natural because the patient introduced the theme, and it shows the patient you were listening.",
w=[("“Now I want to ask some routine questions about alcohol and drugs that I ask everyone.”",
"This is an introduced, accented gate that announces the change. It is excellent, but it does not refer back to the patient’s own words."),
("“How much do you drink in a typical week?”, asked right after a question about sleep",
"This is an abrupt shift. It may work with some patients, but it is not a referred gate."),
("Waiting until the patient raises her own drinking spontaneously before asking anything",
"Waiting for a spontaneous gate is passive. Many patients never volunteer substance use, so the region is lost.")],
x="Facilics is Shea’s study of how the interviewer moves between content regions (gates) and how much time is spent in each. Referred gates are among the most elegant because they make the structure invisible: the patient experiences one conversation, not a series of forms. Expert interviewers bank small mentions (a sibling, a drink, a move) for later use as gates.",
s="Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding",
h="Referred gate = “REFER back to their words.” Bank every passing mention; spend it later as a door."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx othmer rapport strategies", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="Othmer and Othmer describe several strategies for building rapport in the diagnostic interview. Which item belongs to their list?",
a="Find the pain and express compassion",
ae="Othmer and Othmer list strategies such as putting the patient and yourself at ease, finding the pain and expressing compassion, evaluating insight and becoming an ally, showing expertise, establishing leadership, and balancing the roles of interviewer and patient. Finding the pain means locating what actually hurts, which is not always the stated complaint, and responding to it.",
w=[("Mirror the patient’s posture and breathing to create unconscious synchrony",
"Postural mirroring comes from neurolinguistic programming and popular rapport literature, not from Othmer’s diagnostic interview model. Its evidence base is weak."),
("Disclose a comparable personal experience in the first five minutes",
"Early self-disclosure is not part of Othmer’s rapport strategies and often shifts attention to the clinician."),
("Avoid any statement of expertise so the patient feels equal",
"Othmer explicitly lists showing expertise as a rapport strategy: patients relax when they sense the clinician knows the territory.")],
x="Othmer’s model of the interview has four dimensions: rapport, technique, mental status and diagnosis, all operating at once. Their rapport strategies are concrete and teachable. Showing expertise, for example, might mean saying, “Many people with panic attacks fear they are dying; does that match your experience?” This both validates and signals competence.",
s="Othmer & Othmer, The Clinical Interview Using DSM",
h="Othmer’s rapport: “At ease, Pain, Ally, Expert, Leader, Balance” — find where it hurts before you find what it is."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx morrison free speech", m="ask", l=2,
q="Morrison recommends opening the first interview with a period of “free speech.” A highly organized patient has been talking for 6 minutes about his depression, sleep, and job loss. What does Morrison suggest?",
a="Allow the free speech to continue for several minutes before switching to focused questions, intervening mainly to encourage",
ae="Morrison advises letting the patient speak with minimal interruption early on, because the material that patients volunteer, and the order in which they bring it up, are diagnostic. Encouragers and brief clarifications keep it going. Only after this does the interviewer narrow down.",
w=[("Interrupt after the first minute to begin a structured symptom review",
"Early interruption is one of the most common errors in clinical interviewing. It shortens the patient’s narrative and costs information that is never recovered."),
("Remain completely silent throughout so as not to influence the content",
"Total silence makes many patients anxious. Free speech still requires active encouragement and minimal facilitation."),
("Ask the patient to list all symptoms in order of severity",
"This imposes the clinician’s structure and is not free speech.")],
x="The opening narrative tells you what the patient thinks is wrong, how they organize experience, and how coherent their thinking is. It is also when patients form their first impression of whether they are being heard. Research on primary care visits shows that patients who are interrupted early rarely return to their real concern.",
s="Morrison, The First Interview (4th ed.)",
h="Free speech first: “Let the river run before you build the dam.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx why now question", m="ask", l=2,
q="A 45-year-old has had obsessive-compulsive symptoms since adolescence and comes for help for the first time. Which question is most important to the formulation of this presentation?",
a="“You’ve lived with this for nearly 30 years. What made now the time to come?”",
ae="The question “Why now?” identifies the precipitant: what changed in the balance between the patient’s difficulties and their coping. It often uncovers the real chief complaint, such as a new partner, a child imitating rituals, a job threat, or a depressive episode on top of chronic OCD.",
w=[("“What age were you when the symptoms first started?”",
"Age of onset matters for diagnosis and prognosis, but it does not explain the timing of help seeking."),
("“How many hours a day do your rituals take?”",
"Severity matters, but chronic patients often have similar severity for years. The change that brought them is what matters for engagement."),
("“Has anyone in your family had similar problems?”",
"Family history is relevant, but it is not what reveals the precipitant or the patient’s real current request.")],
x="MacKinnon, Michels and Buckley emphasize that the precipitant (“why now?”) and the patient’s expectations of treatment are core to the first interview. The precipitant shapes the treatment contract: the patient whose wife has threatened to leave needs a different first step than the one whose new obsession is about harming his newborn.",
s="MacKinnon, Michels & Buckley, The Psychiatric Interview in Clinical Practice (3rd ed.)",
h="Every chronic story has a fresh chapter: ask “why now?” to find the page they’re on."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx cone technique open to closed", m="ask", l=2,
q="Which sequence best describes the “funnel” or “cone” approach to exploring a symptom?",
a="Open invitation, then focused open questions, then closed questions to pin down specifics, then a summary",
ae="The funnel begins broad (“Tell me about your sleep”), narrows (“What happens when you lie down?”), then closes for specifics (“How many times do you wake?”). Ending with a summary checks accuracy and shows understanding.",
w=[("Closed questions first to set the agenda, then open questions to expand on positives",
"Starting closed narrows the field before you know what matters, and the patient learns to give short answers for the rest of the interview."),
("Only open questions throughout, so the patient never feels led",
"Some data, such as frequency, duration and specific criteria, require closed questions. An interview made only of open questions is often diagnostically incomplete."),
("Leading questions first, to test suggestibility, followed by open questions",
"Leading questions contaminate the data, especially with suggestible patients, children, and those eager to please.")],
x="Different authors use different names for this approach (funnel, cone, open-to-closed). The underlying principle is consistent: let the patient show you the shape of the experience before you measure it. Shea notes that the right level of structure depends on the patient: a rambling patient needs earlier closure; a guarded one needs more open space.",
s="Carlat, The Psychiatric Interview; Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing",
h="Funnel: Wide → Narrow → Pin → Summarize. “Pour the story in at the top; let specifics drip out the bottom.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx meyer life chart", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient has had six depressive episodes over 25 years. You want to understand the relationship between life events, treatment and episodes. Which method, rooted in Adolf Meyer’s psychobiology, is designed for this?",
a="A life chart that maps episodes, life events, treatments and functioning along a timeline",
ae="Meyer developed the life chart to integrate biography and illness. Robert Post and colleagues later formalized the NIMH Life Chart Method for mood disorders, plotting episodes, severity, medications and life events. It reveals cycle acceleration, triggers and treatment responses that a narrative interview misses.",
w=[("A genogram, mapping three generations of family relationships and illnesses",
"A genogram maps family structure and patterns. It is valuable, but its axis is generational, not the patient’s own timeline of episodes and events."),
("A functional analysis of antecedents, behaviors and consequences",
"Functional analysis looks at specific behavioral sequences, usually over minutes to days, not across a 25-year course."),
("A Q-sort of personality descriptors after the interview",
"A Q-sort, such as the SWAP-200, describes personality structure, not illness course.")],
x="Meyer argued that the patient is best understood as a life lived over time, not a cross-section. The life chart puts that into practice. In bipolar disorder especially, it can show kindling-like patterns (early episodes tied to stressors, later ones arising spontaneously) and help with medication decisions based on what actually worked.",
s="Meyer’s psychobiology; Post et al., NIMH Life Chart Method",
h="Meyer’s Map: “MY-er LIFE on a LINE.” Draw it, and the pattern appears."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx macro vs micro structure", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="A supervisor reviews a recorded interview. The trainee asked good individual questions but the patient seemed confused and tired by the end. Which problem most likely explains this?",
a="Poor macrostructure: frequent unannounced jumps between regions, returning to topics already covered",
ae="Expert interviewing works at two levels. Microstructure is the quality of individual questions and responses; macrostructure is the overall flow between regions. A trainee can phrase every question well and still exhaust the patient by darting around, revisiting areas and leaving others half-explored. Shea’s facilic analysis was developed to make this level visible.",
w=[("Excess use of open-ended questions in the opening phase",
"Open questions early are recommended. They do not usually confuse a patient; they help the patient orient."),
("Too many empathic statements throughout",
"Empathic statements, unless false or formulaic, rarely cause confusion or fatigue."),
("Failure to complete the cognitive screening early",
"Cognitive screening placement can matter, but it is not the explanation for a patient feeling disoriented by the interview flow.")],
x="Facilic diagrams (Shea) map the regions visited and the gates between them. When supervisors draw them from recordings, poor interviews look like scribbles, while expert ones show deliberate, well-signposted movement. Patients often cannot say what went wrong, but they feel it as fatigue, confusion or being interrogated.",
s="Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding",
h="Good questions in bad order = a beautiful sentence in a scrambled book."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx chief complaint in own words", m="ask", l=2,
q="Why do MacKinnon, Michels and Buckley and many interview texts insist that the chief complaint be recorded in the patient’s own words?",
a="The words carry the patient’s explanatory model, defensive style and level of insight, which a clinical paraphrase erases",
ae="“My nerves are shot,” “They’re poisoning my food,” and “My wife says I drink too much” all could be paraphrased as anxiety, paranoia or alcohol use. But each phrasing shows how the patient frames the problem, who owns it, and what they hope for. This shapes engagement and the treatment contract.",
w=[("Legal requirements oblige verbatim documentation of the chief complaint",
"There is no general legal requirement for verbatim chief complaints; the reasons are clinical."),
("Patients’ words are always more accurate than clinical terminology",
"Patients’ words are not more accurate. They are more revealing of the patient’s view, which is a different point."),
("It makes the note shorter and easier for billing",
"This has nothing to do with the clinical reasoning behind the convention.")],
x="The chief complaint is often not the real problem. MacKinnon notes that the stated complaint may be a ticket of admission: a socially acceptable reason to come. The interviewer listens for the gap between what is said and what hurts, and keeps the patient’s words as a reference point to return to.",
s="MacKinnon, Michels & Buckley, The Psychiatric Interview in Clinical Practice",
h="Quote the complaint: their words are the fingerprint, your paraphrase is the police sketch."),
]
